Z||000002||lou_dias||02-01-2001||09:19 AM||||I used 87 octane on my rebuilt 3.4 just fine...  Why waste the extra money?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||02-01-2001||12:11 PM||||MPG varies by driving technique and engine tune up.  I routinely get 30 mpg over the road with cruise set at 70 mph. (5 speed)  Milage drops to about 25 mpg around town.  If you are getting 20 mpg, I question how the car is tuned up or how you push the car.<P>I don't do red line shift points.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||02-01-2001||12:48 PM||||High octane gas is just harder to ignite than regular.  You really only need it if you have a high compression/really modified motor.  Or if you've been having problems with pinging or detonation.  I run regular in my 154,000 mile v6 and I get around 21-22 mpg combined.  I like to play boy-racer, tho.  Just hafta redline it a few times if i'm driving   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> can't resist. <P>Oh yeah, if you haven't given it a tune-up lately, try new plugs, plug wires, dist. cap and rotor, o2 sensor, air filter, fuel filter.  If that doesn't help much try replacing the MAT sensor and the coolant temp sensor.  If you have a 180* stat in there, put a 195 back in.  I was getting about 20mpg when I had a 180 in mine, put in a 195 for the winter.  Even though I usually let my car idle for 10 minutes before I drive it the mileage went up.<p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 02-01-2001).]||||reg||
Z||000005||TheFiero64||02-01-2001||03:59 PM||||I only get 20MPG with my 5 speed||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||02-01-2001||06:38 PM||||I don't know what I get lately, haven't paid attention, but I used to consistantly get 33-34 mpg highway, and 24-25mpg city. My record city is 28mpg. All of these were with my Formula 5-speed.||||reg||1
Z||000007||lou_dias||02-02-2001||09:42 AM||||When I picked up my high perf rebuilt 3.4 87 GT 5speed from the Fiero Factory and drove 1150 miles home to Mass, I averaged 26mpg at 80+mph average speed.  That was with a 195 stat.  It has gone down since I've put in the 180 degree stat.  I've noticed that after I've been in traffic and my coolant gets up to 230 degrees and I get on the highway and push it hard and rev it alot and then shut down.  After I've let it sit, and the car is cold again, the power is crazy!  <P>I think pushing your car when it's really hot for a little bit on the highway is a good way to burn out excess carbon build-up thereby cleaning the valves.  So when the engine is cold and the valves are clean so it's breathing ideally, there's a big boost in performance there.<P>It's tough to balance fuel economy, performance and overall engine health.  Basically I think it's just best to drive your car under all conditions to enjoy all the benifits.<P>Ideal:<BR>  1. Cold air in<BR>  2. Clean high flowing valves in and out<BR>  3. hot comustion chamber for total combustion but not enough to ping or detonate<P>however to get #3 you usually sacrifice #1<BR>if you have #1 you usually don't have #3 either so #2 will suffer(carnon buildup)<BR>if you have #2, you have too much of #3 and not enough of #1<P>possible solutions?  cold ram air induction, turbo or supercharger and a heavy duty oil cooler kit and radiator<p>[This message has been edited by lou_dias (edited 02-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||ray b||02-02-2001||06:54 PM||||higher octane gas has less power, if it will run on 87 grade [mine v6 did] will make more power+higher milage than 93 grade.higher grade gas is less explodesise makes less BTU than 87 grade. extra power only in high compression motor that wont run [with out knocking] on lower grade gas.lite ping ok knock is not.<BR>more is not allways better. ray||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bugsy||02-07-2001||09:57 PM||||I run 91 octane always, and I still only get 21 mpg in town....but it jumps up to between 37 and 38 on the road....Maybe I need to take off my lead shoes. Tried it once, had a boring ride  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>86 SE I'm addicted and proud of it<BR>Kevin||||reg||6
Z||000010||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||02-07-2001||11:50 PM||||Traffic sucks here!  My 84 sc auto gets about 20 to 22 MPG.  This is of course in 50 miles of bumper to bumper each day.<BR>When not in traffic, the pedal is either floored or fully released.  <BR>I am sure that my driving style consumes the maximum amount of fuel/mile.  In addition, I have 205/60 15s on Formula rims.  These big tires use more gas.  I floor the car in every turn.  It is amazing how the car just grabs on to the road.  I like to 80 to 85 whenever possible because the duke sounds pretty cool at that speed.  Besides, it's a Fiero so 80 MPH feels like 60 MPH does in other cars.  <BR>I have a 1994 4cylinder ford ranger automatic (2.3L OHC, MFI).  It gets 18 MPG when driven under the same "full throttle/no throttle" regimine.  I have been told that California gas has MTBE and tons of oxygen added to it.  For these reasons, the "power density" of fuels here today is about 75% of what it was in 1984 when my car was made.<P>This would mean that in 1984 my lead foot would still get 25 MPG in heavy traffic.  <P>What does this all mean?  I think it means 20 MPG is pretty good for any car that is more than 15 years old when driven in the traffic and with the fuels of today.  ||||reg||1
